  "summary": "Colorful printed cotton fabrics with distinctive patterns, widely worn as garments and used in East Africa.",
  "description": "Characterized by bold patterns, often featuring proverbs, sayings, or symbolic designs in vibrant colors. Originally influenced by Indonesian batik and later industrial production, serving as cultural expression, communication, and identity across East African communities.",
  "culturalContext": "The kanga (East Africa's printed rectangular pair-cloth) and kitenge (heavier wax print yardage) are Swahili-coast and Central African identity dress: kanga with its jina (proverb) printed along the border — \"Mwanamke ni ardhi\" (woman is the land) — worn as wrap, baby-sling, funeral shroud and courtship message; kitenge (Vlisco lineage, Congolese sapé influence) powers fashion, church-wear and diplomatic gifting. The two prints carry East Africa's social media-before-social-media.",
